Fix a buffer overflow in getAbsolutePath() that aborts every run on glibc.

SWMM passes InpDir (char[MAXFNAME+1], i.e. 260 bytes) as the resolved-name
buffer of realpath(). POSIX requires that buffer to hold PATH_MAX bytes, and
glibc's _FORTIFY_SOURCE checks the object size at the call site: on any
hardened build (which is the nixpkgs default) the solver aborts with
"*** buffer overflow detected ***" while reading the project file, before a
single simulation step runs.

Ask glibc to allocate the buffer instead, then copy the result back inside the
bounds of the caller's array. The neighbouring absolute-path branch has the
same unbounded-copy shape, so it is bounded here too.

The root cause is a Windows MAX_PATH assumption carried into the POSIX branch.
Not yet submitted upstream (USEPA/Stormwater-Management-Model); the Windows
branch is left untouched, so the change is safe to send there as-is.

--
--- a/src/solver/swmm5.c
+++ b/src/solver/swmm5.c
@@ -1590,14 +1590,25 @@
         #ifdef WINDOWS
             GetFullPathName((LPCSTR)fname, (DWORD)size, (LPSTR)absPath, NULL);
         #else
-            realpath(fname, absPath);
+            // POSIX requires the resolved-name buffer passed to realpath() to
+            // hold PATH_MAX bytes; absPath is only MAXFNAME+1. Let glibc
+            // allocate a correctly sized buffer instead, then copy back within
+            // the bounds of absPath.
+            {
+                char* resolved = realpath(fname, NULL);
+                if (resolved != NULL)
+                {
+                    sstrncpy(absPath, resolved, size - 1);
+                    free(resolved);
+                }
+            }
         #endif
     }
 
     // --- otherwise copy fname to absPath
     else
     {
-        sstrncpy(absPath, fname, strlen(fname));
+        sstrncpy(absPath, fname, MIN(strlen(fname), size - 1));
     }
 
     // --- trim file name portion of absPath
